      "Language": "eng",
      "Abstract": {"AbstractText": "A 58-year-old male with a history of prior myocardial infarction, hypertension, and dyslipidemia was admitted due to deteriorating exertional angina. A bare metal stent (Multilink plus™, GUIDANT Corporation, Santa Clara, CA, USA) had been implanted into the proximal left anterior descending artery because of ST-elevation myocardial infarction 7 years earlier. Optical coherence tomography (OCT) showed a disruption of the atherosclerotic neointima overlying the stent. Intravascular imaging studies and pathological studies have shown that neointima within a bare-metal stent often transform into atherosclerotic tissue during an extended period of time. In the current report, OCT demonstrated that a disruption of the atherosclerotic neointima has the potential to cause the development of unstable clinical features. OCT examinations therefore help to understand the pathogenesis of acute coronary syndrome after stent implantation."},
